<h2>The two petitions</h2>
<p>The parties married on 4 December 2014. In 2021, the husband filed a divorce petition on the ground of cruelty before the Family Court at Gurugram (HMA No. 16/2021). On 21 October 2021 that petition was dismissed as withdrawn — without liberty to file afresh. His withdrawal application stated the reason: the parties had never resided together in Gurugram, so the court there had no territorial jurisdiction over the marriage. He then filed a fresh petition at the Dwarka Family Court (HMA No. 23/2022), this time on the grounds of cruelty and desertion.</p>
<p>The wife responded with an application under Order VII Rule 11 CPC, contending that the fresh petition was barred by Order XXIII Rule 1(4): the first petition had been withdrawn without liberty to refile, so a second petition on the same cause could not be instituted. The Family Court dismissed her application on 13 April 2026, and she appealed to the High Court.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>
<p>The Bench dismissed the appeal. Its reasoning turned on what a withdrawal for want of jurisdiction actually abandons. Order XXIII Rule 1(4) penalises a litigant who gives up a claim; it does not penalise a litigant who recognises that the forum chosen could never lawfully decide that claim. As the Court put it, <em>&ldquo;No petition can progress further in a court having no jurisdiction to try the same&rdquo;</em> — and consequently <em>&ldquo;its withdrawal cannot have the effect to non-suit a petitioner whose case is not decided on merits.&rdquo;</em></p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Withdrawal that attracts the bar</strong><br>The claim itself is abandoned, or the case is withdrawn after substantive progress — pleadings joined, evidence underway — without liberty to refile. A fresh petition on the same subject matter is then barred.</div>
  <div class="arrow">vs</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Withdrawal that does not</strong><br>The petition is withdrawn because the court lacked jurisdiction and had not adjudicated anything. The claim was never abandoned on merits, so a fresh petition before the competent court remains open even without express liberty.</div>
</div>
<p>Two further strands supported the conclusion. First, the earlier proceedings had not reached evidence or any form of adjudication; there was nothing an abandonment could be measured against. Second, a rigid application of the bar would have permanently foreclosed the matrimonial claim itself — a result the Bench considered disproportionate, relying on the settled principle that procedure is the handmaid of justice and should not defeat substantive rights. Precedent pressed by the wife was distinguished because, in that case, the first petition had already reached the petitioner&rsquo;s evidence before withdrawal.</p>

<h2>Where the judgment fits</h2>
<p>Order XXIII disputes surface frequently in matrimonial litigation because parties often file where filing is convenient rather than where Section 19 of the Hindu Marriage Act permits. The provision allows a petition where the marriage was solemnised, where the respondent resides, where the parties last resided together, or — for a wife petitioner — where she resides. A petition instituted outside those anchors invites a jurisdictional objection, and withdrawal is often the cleanest exit. This decision confirms that such an exit, taken before any adjudication, does not cost the petitioner the claim.</p>
<div class="note">The decision does not dilute the discipline of Order XXIII for cases withdrawn on the merits, nor does it excuse careless forum selection — the litigant still loses years to the detour. It settles a narrower point: a jurisdictional withdrawal, recorded as such and made before the case progresses, leaves the door to the competent court open.</div>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Before filing under the Hindu Marriage Act, map the Section 19 anchors and keep documentary proof of residence — lease deeds, utility bills, Aadhaar addresses — since jurisdiction is tested on the petition&rsquo;s own averments and supporting record.</p>
  <p>If withdrawal becomes necessary, state the jurisdictional reason in the withdrawal application itself and seek liberty to refile in the same breath; even if liberty is not granted, the recorded reason preserves the argument this judgment vindicates.</p>
  <p>Respondents evaluating an Order VII Rule 11 objection should examine how far the first case travelled: a withdrawal after evidence begins stands on very different footing from one made at the threshold.</p>
</div>
